 the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0, 1, π)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "051e613f-42d1-5b65-a3d0-8638cacfa921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125007 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125255 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125255 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x, y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n$f(2,-2,-1) = 4, f_{x}(2,-2,-1) = 2, f_{y}(2,-2,-1) =-2, f_{z}(2,-2,-1) =-4$.\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125255 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d1a53d9-3ef8-57e5-86e4-66648db12db7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125255 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125286 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125286 #edb/ref 1061 10795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125286 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9a58c4d-a5c9-575a-90d9-009acca5ba22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125286 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125287 #edb/ref 1061 10796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c37b1f0-332e-5bea-b9b7-317063ab4734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125554 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125554 #edb/ref 1061 32184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125554 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f38bdda7-c2e9-534b-8cd8-d22757c051a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125554 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125556 #edb/ref 1061 32300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33df8c71-a53b-56e2-bd72-aceb78b19aa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125571 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125571 #edb/ref 1061 34419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125571 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45087c9a-e754-5204-87c1-5e7e8df40c40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125571 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125572 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125572 #edb/ref 1061 34498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125572 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "75f15315-a63f-503e-9c27-079061856ae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125730 #edb/ref 1064 "We've already seen that if the function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b),$ then the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(a,b) = (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b),\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nz = f(a,b) + (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b).\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is differentiable at $(a,b),$ it can be approximated by its tangent plane at $(a,b)$ *provided that* $(x,y)$ is \"close\" to $(a,b).$ Thus, by replacing $z$ with $f(x,y)$ in the above equation, we get the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b).\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the best linear approximation of the function $f(x,y)$ in an open neighborhood of $(a,b).$\n\nFinally, we can write the right-hand side of the above equation using the gradient vector $\\nabla f(a,b),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx  f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b) \\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b\\rangle\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125730 #edb/ref 1065 2414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125730 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ac689477-f228-5dcd-9ffa-18559bfead85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125730 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125852 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we've been using the fact that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b),$ then\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe can extend this result to higher dimensions. \n\nFor example, if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b, c),$ then \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle,\n$$\n\nfor all $(x,y,z)$ that lie in some open neighborhood of $(a,b,c).$\n\nMore generally, if a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ in the variable $\\mathbf x = (x_1, x_2. \\dots, x_n)$ is differentiable at the point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\Bbb R^n,$ then \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) \\approx f(\\mathbf x_0) +  \\nabla f(\\mathbf x_0) \\cdot (\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0)\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf x$ that lie in some open neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125852 #edb/ref 1065 5547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125852 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "683c6bf8-6cc9-5f40-afe2-0857ca3340c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125852 #edb/ref 1067 "Linearization of Multivariable Functions in General" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125853 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=4-x^2-y^2$ at the point $P(1,1).$ We want to find the best linear approximation to this function, valid in a neighborhood of $P.$\n\nThe partial derivatives of $f$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-2x\\, & ⟹\\, &  & {f}_{x}(1,1)=-2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2y\\, & ⟹\\, &  & {f}_{y}(1,1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivatives are continuous functions at every point, which means that $f$ is differentiable everywhere, and\n\n$$\n\\nabla f (1,1) = \\langle -2, -2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best linear approximation of $f$ in a neighborhood of $(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+(-2)\\cdot (x-1)+(-2)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\  & =2-2x+2-2y+2 \\\\  & =6-2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the diagram below. Notice that in the neighborhood of $(x,y)=(1,1)$, the surface and its tangent plane approximation are very close.\n\n![](images/20/20f06226ec7c21583e56249ea2fe5c65c6127cef8a6e05ee4b1d7521ce49dc38.png)\n\nTherefore, we can use this to approximate values of $f(x,y)$ that are close to $(1,1).$ For example, using our approximation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,1.01) & \\approx 6-2(1.01)-2(1.01) \\\\  & =1.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe actual value of $f$ at this point is $1.9598,$ so our approximation is pretty good!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125853 #edb/ref 1065 5593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125853 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eb2571f8-ee0b-571d-bacf-34a1e469da8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125853 #edb/ref 1067 "Linearizing a Quadratic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263206 #edb/ref 1080 "6.09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263206 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263206 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15ff1e57-06de-5f95-bbfb-858cc1b13fcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263525 #edb/ref 1080 "6.37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263525 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263525 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3b04143b-57f8-52cd-a738-9fc5fdf9d65e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263563 #edb/ref 1080 "5.95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263563 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263563 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3fbbad2d-eebb-5dca-a3ea-e0c4084284e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263591 #edb/ref 1080 "6.22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263591 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263591 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "431cea10-5e99-5fae-b2da-32b333bb11c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265222 #edb/ref 1080 "5.82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265222 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265222 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fcdaec9c-037d-5591-9664-14a70772a823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186263525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265353 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3afc059a-63a4-57e9-911d-aba7f14a7f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265764 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265764 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265764 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f88dd94-3a57-5874-9049-b04644f1e878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265764 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.08,$ $y=1.93,$ and $z=3.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.08,1.93,3.05) & ≈6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.08-1,1.93-2,3.05-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.08,-0.07,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =6+6(0.08)+3(-0.07)+2(0.05) \\\\  & =6+0.48-0.21+0.1 \\\\  & =6.37\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361167 #edb/ref 1080 "0.72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361167 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361167 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c5637de-9cd7-5f67-a225-8bc3edaa10f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361337 #edb/ref 1080 "1.04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361337 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361337 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71543430-b5a0-5270-904e-759c928c2fd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361720 #edb/ref 1080 "0.84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361720 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361720 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c44dcf61-95dc-5835-9ab0-c46e40faab33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361866 #edb/ref 1080 "0.96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361866 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361866 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6886e83-9bbe-5001-aaf4-2aaa990f8f98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361956 #edb/ref 1080 "1.28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361956 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361956 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa86c204-610a-5866-ba1b-3e096237ed19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361866 #edb/ref 17592